            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-d1107568692f20d7bf68\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"The Alps are a major mountain system in south-central Europe, curving in a broad arc from the Ligurian Sea and southeastern France eastward toward Slovenia and the Adriatic. The Alpine Convention identifies an Alpine region spanning eight countries: Austria, France, Germany, Italy, Liechtenstein, Monaco, Slovenia, and Switzerland. The range is distinguished by high, rugged folded mountains, including Mont Blanc, and by its importance as a source area for major rivers such as the Rhine, Rhône, and Po. The Alps formed through tectonic compression and uplift associated with the collision of the Adriatic microplate and Eurasian Plate; mountain passes have long connected northern and Mediterranean Europe.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://courier.unesco.org/en/articles/alpine-europe\",\"https://www.alpconv.org/en/home\",\"https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/fold-mountain/8th-grade/\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"The Alpine Convention’s Alpine region spans eight countries: Austria, France, Germany, Italy, Liechtenstein, Monaco, Slovenia, and Switzerland.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.alpconv.org/en/home\"]},{\"text\":\"The Alps form a broad arc more than 1,000 kilometres long, from the Ligurian Sea toward Slovenia and the Adriatic.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://courier.unesco.org/en/articles/alpine-europe\"]},{\"text\":\"Mont Blanc, between France and Italy, is the highest point in the Alps.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://courier.unesco.org/en/articles/alpine-europe\"]},{\"text\":\"The Rhine, Rhône, and Po rise in the Alps, making the range an important source region for European rivers.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://courier.unesco.org/en/articles/alpine-europe\"]},{\"text\":\"The Alps are fold mountains associated with the collision of the Adriatic microplate and the Eurasian Plate.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/fold-mountain/8th-grade/\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Fold Mountain\",\"url\":\"https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/fold-mountain/8th-grade/\",\"publisher\":\"National Geographic Society\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T17:16:21.898232+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Identifies the Alps as active fold mountains, describes their eight-country extent and Alpine rock types, and attributes their tectonic setting to collision of the Adriatic microplate with the Eurasian Plate.\"},{\"title\":\"The Alpine Convention Who We Are\",\"url\":\"https://www.alpconv.org/en/home\",\"publisher\":\"Alpine Convention\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T17:16:21.898232+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Lists the eight countries included in the Alpine Convention’s Alpine region.\"},{\"title\":\"Alpine Europe\",\"url\":\"https://courier.unesco.org/en/articles/alpine-europe\",\"publisher\":\"The UNESCO Courier\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T17:16:21.898232+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Describes the Alps’ arc-shaped extent, Mont Blanc as the highest point, the Rhine, Rhône and Po as rivers rising there, and the historic role of passes in connecting northern and Mediterranean Europe.\"}],\"research_queries\":[\"site:britannica.com Alps mountain range extent geography\",\"Alps mountain range geography extent official educational source Alps countries geology\",\"site:alpconv.org Alps geography countries mountain range\"],\"notes\":\"The eight-country count follows the Alpine Convention’s regional scope.\",\"unresolved_concerns\":[],\"self_check_complete\":true}"
